-- title:
--   Theorem 3.1(c) — limit points of DCAe are critical
-- statement:
--   Let $f,g,h,\varphi,L,l$ satisfy Assumption 1, let $\delta\in(0,1)$, and let $(x^k)$ be generated by DCAe. If $x^*$ is a limit point of $(x^k)$, then $x^*$ is a critical point of (1):
--
--   $$
--   \bigl[\nabla f(x^*)+\partial g(x^*)\bigr]\cap\partial h(x^*)\neq\emptyset.
--   $$
--
--   This is the subsequential convergence result; it needs neither boundedness of the sequence nor differentiability of $h$.
--
--   **Formalization Note** "Limit point" is a cluster point of the sequence (`MapClusterPt`), i.e. the limit of a subsequence.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: E a finite dimensional real linear space with inner product ⟨·,·⟩ and norm ‖·‖ → any finite-dimensional real inner product space E; reason: the paper uses ⟨·,·⟩ and ‖·‖ on E; same instances
--   - encoding: g : E → (−∞, +∞] and F = f + g − h extended-real → g and F valued in EReal; reason: Lean convention; f and h are real so no ∞ − ∞ occurs; same instances
--   - encoding: x^{−1} := x^0 (initialization of Algorithm 1) → x : ℕ → E and prev x 0 = x 0, prev x (k+1) = x k; reason: the convention is built into prev; same runs
--   - reading: Assumption 1(b) g : E → R ∩ {+∞} → g : E → EReal, proper (never −∞, not identically +∞), lsc, convex; reason: R ∩ {+∞} is empty; the intended codomain is R ∪ {+∞}, as in §2 (p.4)
--   - reading: (9) prints ⟨Lφ(y^k) − ∇f(y^k) + ξ^k, x⟩ → ⟨L∇φ(y^k) − ∇f(y^k) + ξ^k, x⟩; reason: Lφ(y^k) is a number where a vector is needed; the text above Algorithm 1 (∇Lφ(y^k)) and the proof of Theorem 3.1 (L∇φ(y^k)) give the only well-typed reading
--   - reading: β_k is the extrapolation parameter satisfying (8); the proofs on pp.7 and 9 use 0 ≤ β_k < 1 → β_k any real satisfying (8); reason: Algorithm 1 imposes only (8); adding 0 ≤ β_k < 1 would be the weaker reading, and strong convexity of φ with (8) gives the bounds on ‖y^k − x^k‖ the proofs need
--   - reading: critical point: [∇f(x*) + ∂g(x*) ∩ ∂h(x*) ≠ ∅ (unclosed bracket, p.4) → ∃ ξ ∈ ∂h(x*), ξ − ∇f(x*) ∈ ∂g(x*), i.e. [∇f(x*) + ∂g(x*)] ∩ ∂h(x*) ≠ ∅; reason: this is the set the proof of Theorem 3.1(c) shows nonempty, (23) p.8
--   - encoding: x* is a limit point of {x^k} → MapClusterPt xstar atTop x; reason: a cluster point of a sequence in a metric space is the limit of a subsequence
-- source:
--   Phan, Le Thi, DCA based algorithm with extrapolation for nonconvex nonsmooth optimization, arXiv:2106.04743v1, Theorem 3.1(c), p.6

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_DCAExtrap_Assumption1
import Definitions.Def_DCAExtrap_IsDCAeRun
import Definitions.Def_DCAExtrap_IsCriticalPoint

open Filter Topology

namespace DCAExtrap

/-- **Theorem 3.1(c)** (Phan–Le Thi, arXiv:2106.04743v1, p.6). Let `{x^k}` be the sequence
generated by DCAe and suppose Assumption 1 holds. If `x*` is a limit point of `{x^k}`, then `x*`
is a critical point of (1), i.e. `[∇f(x*) + ∂g(x*)] ∩ ∂h(x*) ≠ ∅`.

**Formalization Note.** "Limit point of `{x^k}`" is a cluster point of the sequence
(`MapClusterPt x* atTop x`: the limit of some subsequence). `h` need not be differentiable here.
`β_k` is any real satisfying (8) (no range constraint). -/
theorem theorem_3_1_c {E : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [InnerProductSpace ℝ E]
    [FiniteDimensional ℝ E] {f : E → ℝ} {g : E → EReal} {h : E → ℝ} {φ : E → ℝ} {L l δ : ℝ}
    {x : ℕ → E} {β : ℕ → ℝ} {ξ : ℕ → E}
    (hA1 : Assumption1 f g h φ L l) (hrun : IsDCAeRun f g h φ L l δ x β ξ)
    (xstar : E) (hx : MapClusterPt xstar atTop x) :
    IsCriticalPoint f g h xstar := by sorry

end DCAExtrap
